1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,141
vcs: one repository, many zones, any size.

2
00:00:03,141 --> 00:00:08,232
A version control system written from scratch in Rust, with no git underneath.

3
00:00:08,232 --> 00:00:13,779
This is a short tour of the idea, the numbers we measured, and where it loses to git.

4
00:00:14,780 --> 00:00:18,809
Big repositories mix code, art and documentation in one history.

5
00:00:18,809 --> 00:00:22,775
Everyone downloads everything, even the slice they never touch.

6
00:00:22,775 --> 00:00:28,063
And now many writers, people and agents, publish at the same time to shared storage.

7
00:00:29,060 --> 00:00:30,173
The idea is zones.

8
00:00:30,173 --> 00:00:35,245
Every object, a file chunk, tree, or commit, is named by its hash and stored once.

9
00:00:35,245 --> 00:00:39,142
A zone owns a path prefix and has its own branches and history.

10
00:00:39,142 --> 00:00:43,967
You subscribe only to the zones you need, and the others are never downloaded.

11
00:00:43,967 --> 00:00:49,843
A single manifest, replaced by compare and swap, makes a publish visible at one instant, across

12
00:00:49,843 --> 00:00:50,461
all zones.

13
00:00:51,460 --> 00:00:52,879
Here is the quickstart.

14
00:00:52,879 --> 00:00:58,368
Create two zones, commit once, which is one atomic transaction across zones, publish to a

15
00:00:58,368 --> 00:01:01,328
shared location, and clone only the engine zone.

16
00:01:01,328 --> 00:01:07,064
The assets zone is not downloaded, and it is not deleted either; it is simply not there until

17
00:01:07,064 --> 00:01:07,742
you add it.

18
00:01:08,740 --> 00:01:14,848
It works offline except for sharing, keeps an operation log with undo, and records conflicts as

19
00:01:14,848 --> 00:01:15,942
structured state.

20
00:01:15,942 --> 00:01:21,600
The remote can be a directory, an S3 compatible bucket, or a server, and the protocol is

21
00:01:21,600 --> 00:01:23,593
checked by an executable model.

22
00:01:23,593 --> 00:01:27,644
Large files are chunked and stored once, with no separate tool.

23
00:01:27,644 --> 00:01:31,052
The workspace has 16 crates and 1,774 test functions.

24
00:01:32,050 --> 00:01:33,339
Now the numbers.

25
00:01:33,339 --> 00:01:39,541
On a million file tree, adding everything takes git 215 seconds and vcs 18.9.

26
00:01:39,541 --> 00:01:43,810
That is 11 times faster, from one run on one machine.

27
00:01:44,810 --> 00:01:49,780
Here is every operation on that tree, as git time divided by vcs time.

28
00:01:49,780 --> 00:01:51,555
Above one, vcs is faster.

29
00:01:51,555 --> 00:01:57,447
Diff of a one percent change is 5.3 times faster, and garbage collection 4.3 times.

30
00:01:57,447 --> 00:01:59,151
But below one, git wins.

31
00:01:59,151 --> 00:02:05,541
Adding when nothing changed takes git 0.9 seconds against 6.8 for vcs, and status with one

32
00:02:05,541 --> 00:02:08,380
percent modified is slightly slower too.

33
00:02:09,380 --> 00:02:14,191
Of the 11 operations that can be compared, vcs is faster on 8 and git on 3.

34
00:02:14,191 --> 00:02:20,284
The clone row is not like for like, because a local path clone is the fast case for git too, so

35
00:02:20,284 --> 00:02:21,887
treat that one with care.

36
00:02:22,890 --> 00:02:24,495
Zones also save bytes.

37
00:02:24,495 --> 00:02:29,382
On a fifty zone repository, a full clone downloads 189.5 megabytes.

38
00:02:29,382 --> 00:02:36,093
When the zones are chosen from your usage profile, it downloads 37, which is 80 percent less

39
00:02:36,093 --> 00:02:38,427
in 1.16 seconds instead of 2.87.

40
00:02:39,430 --> 00:02:40,274
Honest limits.

41
00:02:40,274 --> 00:02:44,616
This is early research software and the on disk format may still change.

42
00:02:44,616 --> 00:02:49,922
Every benchmark is one machine, one build and a small number of runs, and git still wins

43
00:02:49,922 --> 00:02:50,706
several rows.

44
00:02:50,706 --> 00:02:56,374
Windows is only compile checked, the AI merge helpers are tested with mocks, and vcs uses more

45
00:02:56,374 --> 00:02:58,907
memory than git on add, publish and clone.

46
00:02:59,910 --> 00:03:00,192
vcs.

47
00:03:00,192 --> 00:03:05,548
The code, the raw benchmark data and the full documentation are in the repo.

